  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nThe Central Plains is wedged between a mid-level trough to the east \nand a ridge to the west with surface high pressure overhead. Light \nwinds and clear skies will allow temperatures to cool into the low \nto mid 60s by daybreak. Patchy fog may develop in low-lying areas \nand river valleys this morning, but do not expect dense fog and it \nwill burn off shortly after sunrise. While not as nice as yesterday, \ntoday will still be seasonably pleasant with light winds, sunny \nskies, and highs in the mid to upper 80s. Patchy fog could develop \nagain tonight into early Monday morning, mainly across east central \nKansas where surface winds are progged to be lighter. Even so, \nelevated winds just off the surface may lead to enough mixing to \nprevent fog development. \n\nLow-level winds become southerly on Monday as the mid-level ridge \nexpands over the Central Plains, leading to highs in the upper 80s \nto mid 90s both Monday and Tuesday. A strong shortwave moving across \nsouthern Canada and the Northern Plains will flatten the ridge and \nshunt a surface boundary into the area late Tuesday. This boundary \nmeanders around the region through the end of the week with passing \nperturbations leading to periodic chances for showers and storms. \nThe highest chances come during the overnight hours Tue-Thu night. \nThe severe parameter space could support some strong to severe \nstorms during this timeframe, but details are hard to decipher at \nthis range. Temperature spreads widen with a 5-10 degree difference \nin NBM 25th-75th percentiles for highs, depending on daytime \nprecipitation and cloud cover. The ridge does expand further to end \nthe week, favoring hotter conditions with heat index values \npotentially around 100 degrees again next weekend.",
